1
00:00:00,007 --> 00:00:09,674
Narrator: One prescription claim, start to finish, on the real dashboards. It starts with a plan. Theo Brandvold builds plans for the insurer.

2
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:15,277
Theo Brandvold: Standard Rx, 2026.

3
00:00:18,664 --> 00:00:21,294
Narrator: Amounts on this form are in cents.

4
00:00:21,656 --> 00:00:27,965
Theo Brandvold: Five hundred deductible. Seventy-eight hundred out-of-pocket. Sixteen thousand for a family.

5
00:00:33,335 --> 00:00:33,972
Theo Brandvold: Create.

6
00:00:35,899 --> 00:00:51,664
Narrator: His node stores the plan on the sequencer, the network's ledger, and publishes it with a signed request. When a member enrolls, the claims engine copies her limits from this copy and binds her to this plan's formulary, copay and dispensing fee.

7
00:00:52,134 --> 00:00:52,962
Delphine Okerlund: Two plans.

8
00:00:54,972 --> 00:01:03,531
Delphine Okerlund: Standard. Five hundred deductible, seventy-eight hundred out-of-pocket, ten-dollar base copay. That's the one we budgeted.

9
00:01:24,573 --> 00:01:27,249
Delphine Okerlund: Effective today. Cap of twenty-five.

10
00:01:28,817 --> 00:01:29,545
Delphine Okerlund: Submit.

11
00:01:31,124 --> 00:01:45,950
Narrator: When she submitted, her browser made an Ed25519 key pair and kept the private half in page memory. The insurer got the public half with the application. That key will sign Fernhill's agreement later.

12
00:01:47,103 --> 00:01:49,991
Delphine Okerlund: Activation code. Copied, and saved.

13
00:01:51,154 --> 00:01:53,720
Delphine Okerlund: They want our CP 575.

14
00:01:54,088 --> 00:01:55,622
Delphine Okerlund: I have it. Uploading.

15
00:01:59,512 --> 00:02:02,545
Theo Brandvold: Fernhill Test Works. Letter's on file.

16
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,745
Theo Brandvold: Name and EIN match the application.

17
00:02:08,609 --> 00:02:09,462
Theo Brandvold: Approved.

18
00:02:11,875 --> 00:02:15,495
Delphine Okerlund: It won't let me sign until I scroll to the end. Fine.

19
00:02:21,731 --> 00:02:24,632
Delphine Okerlund: Benefits Director. Attest. Sign.

20
00:02:25,758 --> 00:02:46,200
Narrator: Her key signs the agreement, and the insurer checks it against her application's public key and adds its own signature. The sequencer checks both, the insurer's against a key pinned in its own configuration, not one carried in the document. Then the insurer starts a separate employer node for Fernhill.

21
00:02:46,566 --> 00:02:50,747
Delphine Okerlund: Node address. Admin token, shown once. Copying it now.

22
00:02:55,572 --> 00:02:56,464
Delphine Okerlund: Token's in.

23
00:02:59,852 --> 00:03:03,679
Delphine Okerlund: One group for the lab staff. Standard plan, effective today.

24
00:03:07,571 --> 00:03:18,054
Narrator: This node serves Fernhill alone. It accepts a group only on a plan Fernhill bought. The group ID stays here; only its hash ever leaves.

25
00:03:19,158 --> 00:03:22,349
Delphine Okerlund: Node address. Token. Passphrase, twice.

26
00:03:32,203 --> 00:03:33,079
Delphine Okerlund: Lab staff.

27
00:03:38,921 --> 00:03:41,155
Delphine Okerlund: Six rows. Same as the roster.

28
00:03:41,524 --> 00:03:43,647
Delphine Okerlund: Codes expire end of next month.

29
00:03:45,777 --> 00:04:08,911
Narrator: This tool is meant for Fernhill's own computer. It hashes each employee ID and family ID with a secret seed it keeps for this group, and sends Fernhill's node only those hashes. The node issues one code per person. The tool adds an eight-digit PIN; the node gets only a hash of code and PIN together.

30
00:04:09,271 --> 00:04:12,704
Delphine Okerlund: Codes go in the welcome letters. PINs by text.

31
00:04:13,507 --> 00:04:25,232
Narrator: A few days later, Avery Quillfeather, a lab technician at Fernhill, has a letter with a code and a text with a PIN. Here the member node on port 4001 plays her phone.

32
00:04:25,598 --> 00:04:27,067
Avery Quillfeather: Okay. New enrollment.

33
00:04:28,342 --> 00:04:29,888
Avery Quillfeather: A PIN for the phone first.

34
00:04:31,854 --> 00:04:34,136
Avery Quillfeather: Code from the letter. PIN from the text.

35
00:04:36,176 --> 00:04:38,616
Avery Quillfeather: And a recovery PIN. Something I won't forget.

36
00:04:39,778 --> 00:04:42,656
Avery Quillfeather: Cardholder ID, from the card. Name. Birthday.

37
00:04:49,052 --> 00:04:54,465
Narrator: First the phone makes a random enrollment key. It never leaves the phone in one piece.

38
00:04:55,042 --> 00:05:05,991
Narrator: Then it redeems the code: the code and one hash of code and PIN go to the insurer, which forwards them, signed, to the node that issued the code.

39
00:05:06,344 --> 00:05:19,000
Narrator: Fernhill's node checks the hash, marks the code used, and answers with group, plan and family hashes. The insurer keeps the family hash and passes the other two to the phone.

40
00:05:24,467 --> 00:05:26,602
Narrator: Here is what the phone just computed.

41
00:05:26,975 --> 00:05:47,759
Narrator: One function does all the hashing. H is Poseidon, a hash that is cheap inside a proof, working modulo p, a prime just above two to the two hundred fifty-four. enc packs text into field elements, thirty-one bytes each; hs hashes a string.

42
00:05:48,124 --> 00:06:18,188
Narrator: The phone draws a salt, two hundred fifty-six random bits; sigma is its hash. Each identity field is hashed with sigma: f-zero the cardholder ID, f-two and f-three the names, f-four the enrollment key, K. The birth date, f-one, goes in as one number. f-five is g, the group hash from the redeemed code, with no salt.

43
00:06:18,552 --> 00:06:31,199
Narrator: Seven field elements go in, a zero that tags a member wallet, then f-zero through f-five. One comes out: W, the wallet ID.

44
00:06:31,566 --> 00:06:43,429
Narrator: f-zero to f-four never leave the phone; f-five came from the insurer at redemption. W goes out, and Fernhill never sees it.

45
00:06:43,815 --> 00:06:58,147
Narrator: Enrolling sends the insurer the wallet ID. The insurer checks that the code was redeemed and no other wallet used it, then sends the engine a signed approval: the wallet, its plan, its group and family hashes.

46
00:06:58,514 --> 00:07:14,442
Narrator: The same call splits the enrollment key, once per recovery card, into two random-looking shares. The card gets one. The insurer gets the other, beside a slow hash of the recovery PIN, the salt, and the group and plan hashes.

47
00:07:17,333 --> 00:07:23,011
Narrator: Then the phone compiles its circuits and starts the registration proof.

48
00:07:23,366 --> 00:07:26,553
Narrator: While the phone proves, here is what it proves.

49
00:07:26,931 --> 00:07:52,233
Narrator: First, the tool every tree here uses. MR is a Merkle path root: start at a leaf, ell, and at each level hash it with one sibling, s-i. The bit b-i says which side. z-zero is zero, and each z-k hashes two copies of the one below, so an empty tree's root is known in advance.

50
00:07:52,603 --> 00:08:29,901
Narrator: Now the proof. t is the domain tag; zero means member, so the circuit rebuilds W from the six private values. The wallet registry and the lifecycle tree each have height eleven, a thousand and twenty-four slots. Line two: a zero at W's slot hashes up to today's roots, so the slot is empty. Line three writes W with an empty inventory and a nonce of zero, and W with state one, registered. Only x is public: W and four roots.

51
00:08:30,261 --> 00:09:03,064
Narrator: The sequencer checks every proof at one gate. At boot it compiled this program itself, in a separate verifier process, and checked the key's hash against a pinned value; this take pins one, and without a pin it only warns. A proof passes only if it verifies, its old roots are the ledger's current roots, and its new roots match the sequencer's own recomputation: the next free slot for a registration, or the wallet's own slot, nonce plus one, for an update.

52
00:09:03,419 --> 00:09:12,066
Narrator: Only then does anything change. And while the phone proved, it held the proving lease, so nothing else could move those roots.

53
00:09:12,435 --> 00:09:16,151
Avery Quillfeather: Write your two recovery cards now. Keep one at home. Okay.

54
00:09:16,517 --> 00:09:27,098
Narrator: Each card holds one share of her enrollment key and a random ID, and no wallet ID. The wallet ID on screen is an address, not a secret.

55
00:09:27,456 --> 00:09:27,947
Avery Quillfeather: Done.

56
00:09:29,784 --> 00:09:47,008
Narrator: No product screen shows this part, so this card is drawn from the network's own answers. The registration proof was accepted. Then the phone sent the engine a second proof: that it knows the six values behind W, without sending them.

57
00:09:47,399 --> 00:10:04,247
Narrator: The engine opens a record only for a wallet the insurer approved and the ledger shows registered. It took Avery's limits from her plan, marked her eligible with an admin write, not a proof, and queued her first credential, which the phone proved too.

58
00:10:04,633 --> 00:10:07,671
Narrator: Two equations hold the engine's record for Avery.

59
00:10:08,036 --> 00:10:34,214
Narrator: A is the accumulator commitment, one hash over seven numbers: y, the plan year; D, the deductible, and d, paid toward it; M, the out-of-pocket maximum, and o, paid; B, the annual benefit maximum, and b, what the plan has paid. All in cents. On day one, everything paid is zero.

60
00:10:34,600 --> 00:11:00,077
Narrator: A credential carries one number for its data, a forest root: the hash of six tree roots, each of height eight. At registration only access control has leaves. Leaf zero is her eligibility record, which binds her to her plan: its copay and the root of its formulary. Leaf one twenty-seven is A. The other trees are empty: z-seven.

61
00:11:00,451 --> 00:11:08,373
Narrator: In the wallet registry, this whole credential is one number: the inventory root inside her leaf.

62
00:11:11,346 --> 00:11:14,390
Avery Quillfeather: Providers. Dr. Fallowmere. That's who I saw.

63
00:11:15,553 --> 00:11:17,275
Avery Quillfeather: Primary care. Done.

64
00:11:18,903 --> 00:11:40,994
Narrator: The engine checks that Dr. Fallowmere is a credentialed, active prescriber and records her in Avery's credential, as leaf zero of the member tree. The phone proves that update, then posts the link to the sequencer, signed with its wallet key. Avery joins the doctor's list as a wallet ID, with no name.

65
00:11:44,437 --> 00:11:46,722
Dr. Miriam Fallowmere: New patient on my list. That's her.

66
00:11:48,274 --> 00:11:50,073
Dr. Miriam Fallowmere: Kettleby's. Her usual.

67
00:11:53,529 --> 00:11:57,164
Dr. Miriam Fallowmere: Allopurinol, a hundred milligrams. Start low.

68
00:11:59,549 --> 00:12:20,491
Narrator: Before she writes it, her node asks the network what Avery would pay today. It may: Avery named her as primary care. The answer comes from the same arithmetic the fill will prove. She would pay $14.18, all of it toward the deductible, and the drug is covered on her plan with no prior authorization.

69
00:12:20,863 --> 00:12:23,438
Dr. Miriam Fallowmere: One a day, after a meal. Thirty.

70
00:12:27,025 --> 00:12:29,632
Dr. Miriam Fallowmere: No refills until I see her uric acid.

71
00:12:31,418 --> 00:12:32,023
Dr. Miriam Fallowmere: Send.

72
00:12:33,879 --> 00:12:45,891
Narrator: Her node never received a name. It encrypts the e-script twice, for the pharmacy and for Avery's phone, then asks the engine, which runs inside the sequencer's process, to record it.

73
00:12:46,265 --> 00:13:12,746
Narrator: The engine accepts: Avery named her as primary care, both providers are in network, and the drug needs no prior authorization or step therapy. Then it makes four proofs: the doctor's identity, made with a key it still holds for her; eligibility; formulary access; and the message. It throws them away; nobody verifies them yet.

74
00:13:13,118 --> 00:13:16,454
Narrator: The ledger gets only a messaging audit event.

75
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:21,851
Walt Kettleby: Allopurinol, hundred milligram, thirty.

76
00:13:23,261 --> 00:13:25,222
Walt Kettleby: Filled. Now we wait for her.

77
00:13:25,590 --> 00:13:35,212
Narrator: Fill is a status change. No proof, no money. This page keeps Dispense disabled until the member is verified.

78
00:13:35,567 --> 00:13:37,275
Walt Kettleby: Go ahead and tap your phone.

79
00:13:37,642 --> 00:13:38,327
Avery Quillfeather: One sec.

80
00:13:40,956 --> 00:14:10,806
Narrator: The phone asks the sequencer for an attestation, signed with its wallet key. The sequencer signs one line with its Ed25519 key: the wallet ID, the registry root, the lifecycle state, a global-state field that is zero for now, the time, a one-day expiry and a random nonce. It is meant to travel by NFC tap; here, by clipboard.

81
00:14:12,236 --> 00:14:16,632
Narrator: The tap is simulated: copied from the phone, pasted here.

82
00:14:18,902 --> 00:14:21,426
Walt Kettleby: There you are. One ready to go.

83
00:14:22,981 --> 00:14:44,356
Narrator: The node checked the sequencer's signature against a key it already holds, and the expiry. With that attestation, not a bare wallet ID, it may read her claim history, which is empty. Dispense unlocks, but that gate lives in this page; the server doesn't ask for the tap.

84
00:14:44,719 --> 00:14:45,804
Walt Kettleby: Running it through.

85
00:14:46,485 --> 00:15:15,283
Narrator: Dispense sends the engine the prescription and the quantity, and no price. The engine prices the fill itself: the drug's NADAC price times the quantity, plus her plan's dispensing fee. It refuses a terminated member or a same-day repeat of this drug at this pharmacy, then makes three proofs: one for the pharmacy's identity, one for the script's move to dispensed, and one for the fill.

86
00:15:15,651 --> 00:15:36,451
Narrator: Before any money, the fill circuit checks the pharmacy, finds the prescription and its filled status, matches the drug to the script and formulary, checks the quantity and that the accumulator is Avery's. For now, the engine still holds the pharmacy's key for that first check.

87
00:15:36,834 --> 00:15:42,251
Narrator: Inside that proof, the money starts from her record, A.

88
00:15:42,628 --> 00:16:20,133
Narrator: T is the total: the drug's NADAC price times the quantity, plus her plan's dispensing fee. Here, $14.18, so on the board T is 1,418. inDed, the in-deductible flag, is true while a deductible is unmet. r-D is what is left of it: all of it, $500.00. p-D, this fill's deductible portion, is T or r-D, whichever is smaller. The whole total fits, so the rest, a, is zero.

89
00:16:20,494 --> 00:16:39,932
Narrator: c is the copay, and it comes from her plan: ten dollars. c-rem is the copay on what remains, so zero. p-norm is the usual copay, after the deductible. She is still in it, so her base pay is p-D plus zero: the whole total.

90
00:16:40,298 --> 00:16:57,167
Narrator: Then the benefit cap, the out-of-pocket cap and the family rules. None binds today. So P, what she pays, is the total; Q, what the plan pays, is zero.

91
00:16:57,998 --> 00:17:01,011
Walt Kettleby: That's $14.18 today.

92
00:17:01,376 --> 00:17:04,042
Avery Quillfeather: Oh. I thought it was a ten-dollar copay.

93
00:17:04,398 --> 00:17:14,673
Walt Kettleby: It will be, once you've met your deductible. Until then you pay what the drug costs, and this one's a generic. All of it counts toward your five hundred.

94
00:17:15,030 --> 00:17:16,666
Avery Quillfeather: Okay. Here you go.

95
00:17:17,033 --> 00:17:20,111
Walt Kettleby: Take it after a meal, with plenty of water.

96
00:17:20,473 --> 00:17:39,074
Narrator: The badge says Settlement Complete. More exactly: the fill proof stayed in the engine, and it takes one more proof, from Avery's phone, to put the claim on the ledger. Payment Received is the pharmacy's own note that she paid; it changes nothing on the network.

97
00:17:39,767 --> 00:17:44,080
Narrator: Walt was right about the deductible. Here is where the proof says so.

98
00:17:44,443 --> 00:17:54,298
Narrator: Kappa, the deductible credit, is the smaller of what she pays and the deductible portion. Both are the whole total, so all of it counts.

99
00:17:54,657 --> 00:18:17,088
Narrator: fc hashes the facts of the fill: prescription hash, pharmacy wallet, drug-code hash, quantity times a thousand, ingredient cost, fee and date of service. S hashes fc with the money, P, Q and kappa. That settlement commitment goes into the prescription's own transaction tree.

100
00:18:17,450 --> 00:18:40,703
Narrator: The proof also outputs F-prime, her new eligibility forest root. Before recording anything, the engine rebuilds it from her own year and limits, with kappa, P and Q added to what she has paid, along the same path at leaf one twenty-seven. If it does not equal F-prime, the engine throws and records nothing.

101
00:18:41,068 --> 00:18:48,138
Narrator: Then it keeps the outputs and drops the proof. Nobody outside the engine verifies a fill proof yet.

102
00:18:48,511 --> 00:18:58,915
Narrator: No screen shows the next part either. The engine queued a wallet update for Avery and parked the claim against it. Her phone checks for work every twenty seconds.

103
00:18:59,301 --> 00:19:02,536
Narrator: The claim reaches the ledger through a proof the phone makes.

104
00:19:02,900 --> 00:19:36,831
Narrator: First the phone checks the engine's work: it rebuilds the credential from the leaves it was sent, F-star being their forest root, in slot zero of a fresh inventory, and refuses unless that gives C-new. The update carries no amounts; this checks only that the root adds up. Then the proof: W from the six private values, a lifecycle state, ell, that is not four, which means revoked, and her leaf moving from C-old and nonce n to C-new and n plus one.

105
00:19:37,398 --> 00:19:47,038
Narrator: Accepted, at the same gate as her registration. The sequencer attached the claim the engine parked for exactly this wallet and this root.

106
00:19:47,406 --> 00:19:58,494
Narrator: One caveat: the amounts ride along from the engine, and nothing outside the engine checks them against the fill proof yet. The engine did check its own record against that proof first.

107
00:19:59,268 --> 00:20:02,878
Narrator: Accepting that proof, the sequencer wrote two records.

108
00:20:03,263 --> 00:20:30,474
Narrator: First, the audit event. E-k hashes eleven values: the wallet, four roots, the sequence number k, a registration flag, the drug-code hash, P, Q, and op, one for a fill. Chi-k hashes the previous link with E-k, from the hash of zero. Change any past event's hash and every later link stops matching.

109
00:20:30,872 --> 00:20:57,475
Narrator: Second, the claim leaf. L-j hashes nine values: Avery's wallet, the drug-code hash, both providers' wallets, the hashed date, P, Q, op and kappa. It is appended at the next free leaf of a tree twenty-four levels deep, with room for more than eight million claims, whose root is stored with every batch.

110
00:20:57,867 --> 00:21:13,113
Narrator: Neither record holds her name. Both hold the money. And the drug is not hidden, by design: beside both records, the sequencer keeps its code and name in plain text, so the plan can track what it spends on each drug.

111
00:21:14,802 --> 00:21:22,416
Narrator: This is the sequencer's own dashboard. In this boot it builds a batch every thirty seconds, from at most two accepted proofs.

112
00:21:24,892 --> 00:21:37,577
Narrator: This batch holds Avery's update. Its record carries the claims root. Two replicas copy the sequencer's receipt and new roots for every accepted proof; they don't re-check the proofs yet.

113
00:21:40,171 --> 00:22:04,081
Narrator: The batch proof re-verifies accepted proofs inside a circuit. wrapSingle wraps one proof and outputs its roots and counts. wrapPair takes two and checks they chain: the first one's new roots are the second one's old roots. mergeBatches would join batches the same way, but at two proofs per batch it never runs, and no chain proof runs above it yet.

114
00:22:04,442 --> 00:22:08,262
Narrator: Anyone can check a batch proof against a key they compile themselves.

115
00:22:09,711 --> 00:22:12,100
Theo Brandvold: Daily checks. Chain integrity.

116
00:22:16,296 --> 00:22:18,344
Theo Brandvold: Financial balance. Good.

117
00:22:18,718 --> 00:22:40,036
Narrator: Chain integrity re-walks the chain from the hash of zero, recomputing each link from the stored event hashes. Financial balance checks, fill by fill, that patient pay plus plan pay equals cost plus fee. The sequencer's own version also compares the chain's totals with the claim ledger's; this tab doesn't call it.

118
00:22:42,162 --> 00:22:42,954
Avery Quillfeather: There it is.

119
00:22:43,304 --> 00:22:53,400
Narrator: The phone reads its claims from the sequencer, signed with its wallet key. The card shows the drug, the split, and the pharmacy as a wallet hash.

120
00:22:54,538 --> 00:22:56,453
Avery Quillfeather: So that went toward my deductible.

121
00:22:56,798 --> 00:23:24,518
Narrator: These totals are sums over her claim leaves. All along, her name, birth date and cardholder ID stayed on her phone. Fernhill never learned her wallet ID. The doctor saw a short hash, the pharmacy a wallet ID inside a signed attestation. The insurer holds the salt and one key share per card, released only for the right recovery PIN.

122
00:23:24,473 --> 00:23:39,063
Narrator: Two parts are unfinished. Nobody outside the engine verifies the fill proof yet, and the amounts on the ledger are the engine's word, tied to the phone's proof but not to the fill proof. That is the claim, as the code runs it today.
